# S4 — the arithmetic survives, the claim does not (lara-syntax@0.3)
#
# S2's artifact, with one leaf added: an audit finding that the two accuracy
# cells were produced under different evaluation settings. Everything else is
# unchanged — the same two cells, the same certificate, the same bridge.
#
# What the added leaf does, and does not do:
#
#   * a1, the STRICT ord@1 step, is untouched. It stays `in`. Nothing in this
#     artifact attacks it, and by construction nothing could: its conclusion
#     num_lt(0.71, 0.74) heads no contrary pair, because a sound backend can
#     never certify a conflicting member of the family over the same literals
#     (lean/Lara/Ord.lean). 0.71 < 0.74 is not up for debate.
#
#   * a2, the DEFEASIBLE bridge, loses its binding premise. The audit's
#     conclusion settings_differ(...) is declared contrary to the binding leaf
#     e3, so x1 UNDERMINES a2 at its binding premise. With no defender, x1 is
#     `in` and a2 is `out`.
#
#   * c1 — "sys_new outperforms sys_base" — is therefore DEFEATED, not gap: a
#     complete argument for it exists and was beaten, which is a stronger and
#     more useful reading than having no argument at all.
#
# This is the factivity firewall doing its job in the grounded semantics. The
# certificate discharged the arithmetic and nothing else; the disputed content
# — that these two numbers answer the same question — was never inside the
# certificate, so the attack lands on the layer that actually asserted it. You
# cannot argue with the arithmetic. You argue with the binding, and here the
# argument succeeds.
#
# WHAT CHANGED AT @0.3, and why S4 is the sharpest test of it. a1 and a2 are now
# generated from a `comparison` block, exactly as in S2 — and the attack below
# still lands, on the same argument at the same premise, producing a
# byte-identical Unit. Generated structure is ordinary structure: the elaborator
# emits an argument by the policy's defeasible bridge rule, so it is as
# attackable as a hand-written one. A surface that produced something the critic
# could not reach would have quietly moved a defeasible step out of the
# argument.
#
# The attack is also spelled differently, and that is the second thing S4 tests.
# It reads `a2.binding.leaf`, not `a2.1.leaf`. Under @0.2 the author had to
# count premises into an argument they did not write; now the bridge rule labels
# its premises and the attack names the one it disputes. Both spellings compile
# to the same Undermine at the same premise — the label is resolved against the
# rule and never reaches the Unit — so this is a readability change with no
# semantic content, which is exactly what makes it safe.
#
# Expected verdict is at the bottom of this file (golden oracle).

artifact ord_setting_demo at sha256:5454545454545454545454545454545454545454545454545454545454545454
policy ord-setting-v1
use backends [ord@1]

# --- The comparative claim under attack -------------------------------------------------------

claim c1
  nl      = "sys_new outperforms sys_base on ImageNet-val accuracy (0.74 vs 0.71)"
  formal  = better(sys_new, sys_base, accuracy, imagenet_val)
  binding = { author = alice, rationale = "Ordered comparison of two reported accuracy cells.", audit-status = disputed }

# --- The two measured cells the certificate consults ------------------------------------------
# Neither cell is disputed. The audit does not claim either number is wrong.

leaf e1 : reports(exp1, score_cell(sys_base, accuracy, imagenet_val, 0.71))
  kind       = observed
  provenance = ai-executed
  refs       = [evidence/tables/accuracy.md#row=sys_base]

leaf e2 : reports(exp1, score_cell(sys_new, accuracy, imagenet_val, 0.74))
  kind       = observed
  provenance = ai-executed
  refs       = [evidence/tables/accuracy.md#row=sys_new]

# The binding — the attack surface. This is the only place the artifact asserts that the two
# numbers are comparable, and it is an attestation, not a measurement.
#
# It is declared here rather than generated by the comparison block, and S4 is the reason the
# form works that way: if the sugar synthesized this leaf, the thing x1 attacks below would be
# evidence the compiler invented and nobody signed.

leaf e3 : comparison_setup(sys_new, sys_base, accuracy, imagenet_val, 0.74, 0.71)
  kind       = attested
  provenance = user
  refs       = [evidence/tables/accuracy.md#caption]

# The audit's own evidence.

leaf e4 : audits_settings(aud1, sys_new, sys_base, accuracy, imagenet_val)
  kind       = observed
  provenance = ai-executed
  refs       = [evidence/audits/settings.md#sec=resolution]

# --- The comparison: S2's block, unchanged ------------------------------------------------------
# a1 (strict, certified, unattacked) and a2 (the defeasible bridge, defeated below) plus the
# sub-claim c2 they turn on. The block is identical to S2's — S4's whole diff against S2 is the
# audit leaf, the critic, and the attack edge.

comparison : better(sys_new, sys_base) on accuracy @ imagenet_val
  relation = strictly-better
  recheck  = a1
  bridge   = a2
  result   = e2
  baseline = e1
  binding  = e3
  claims c2
    nl      = "The reported baseline accuracy {cell e1} is strictly below the reported system accuracy {cell e2}"
    binding = { author = alice, audit-status = reviewed }
  supports c1

# --- The critic -------------------------------------------------------------------------------

arg x1 : challenges(e3) by setting_audit(sys_new, sys_base, accuracy, imagenet_val, 0.74, 0.71, aud1)

# The typed edge: a2's `binding` premise is the binding leaf, and x1's conclusion is its declared
# contrary. Undermine, not rebut — the target is a leaf occurrence inside the support term,
# not the bridge's conclusion.
#
# `a2.binding.leaf` names that premise by the label ord-setting-v1 gives it. `a2.1.leaf` is the
# same position written positionally and remains legal; the elaborator resolves both to the same
# premise slot. The name is worth having precisely because a2 is generated — counting premises
# into an argument the author did not write is the fragility this surface removes.

undermine x1 a2.binding.leaf

status c1
status c2

# ==============================================================================================
# EXPECTED VERDICT (golden oracle)
# ==============================================================================================
# Compiled AF: a1 (strict, unattacked), a2 (bridge, undermined at its binding premise — slot 1 —
# by x1), x1 (unattacked).
# Grounded labelling: a1 -> in, x1 -> in, a2 -> out.
# Claim status:
#   c1 -> defeated (a2 is a complete argument for c1 and every such argument is out)
#   c2 -> justified (a1 is in; the arithmetic is untouched by the attack)
